{"id":"21e0e744-7277-4571-8826-b8b1fca97d72","object":"chat.completion","created":1784807221,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    tokens = case[\\\"tokens\\\"]\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"]\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\\\"score\\\"], c[\\\"start\\\"], c[\\\"call_id\\\"]))\\n    selected = []\\n    for call in sorted_calls:\\n        start = call[\\\"start\\\"]\\n        end = call[\\\"end\\\"]\\n        overlap = False\\n        for s in selected:\\n            if not (end <= s[\\\"start\\\"] or s[\\\"end\\\"] <= start):\\n                overlap = True\\n                break\\n        if not overlap:\\n            selected.append(call)\\n    selected_sorted = sorted(selected, key=lambda c: c[\\\"start\\\"])\\n    selected_ids = [c[\\\"call_id\\\"] for c in selected_sorted]\\n    result = list(tokens)\\n    for call in reversed(selected_sorted):\\n        start = call[\\\"start\\\"]\\n        end = call[\\\"end\\\"]\\n        result[start:end] = call[\\\"replacement_tokens\\\"]\\n    return {\\\"selected_ids\\\": selected_ids, \\\"tokens\\\": result}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that returns a JSON-serializable object. Based on the task description: \"Apply accepted API calls to text while preserving registered span order, rejecting overlaps, and keeping all eligible calls.\" The case has tokens and calls. We need to select calls that do not overlap, prioritizing by descending score, then start, then call ID. Then apply replacements from right to left to avoid offset shifts. Return selected IDs and the interleaved token stream.\n\nFrom fixture: tokens array of strings, calls array with call_id, start, end, score, replacement_tokens. The start and end are indices into tokens (presumably inclusive? Usually start inclusive, end exclusive? But example: start=1, end=3 replaces tokens[1], tokens[2]? Actually replacement_tokens is a list, likely multiple tokens. We need to replace the span from start to end (exclusive) with the replacement_tokens. The calls have score, we rank by descending score, then by start ascending? \"Rank candidates by descending score, start position, then call ID.\" So sort by (-score, start, call_id). Then greedily accept: if the span does not overlap any already selected span, accept it. Overlap means any index in [start, end) overlaps? Since we are using indices, define overlap if intervals intersect. For a call with start, end (end exclusive), two intervals overlap if not (end1 <= start2 or end2 <= start1). We'll reject any that overlaps previously selected.\n\nThen order accepted calls by source start for reporting. Then apply replacements from right to left so earlier offsets do not shift. The final interleaved token stream: start with original tokens, then for each accepted call in decreasing order of start (right to left), replace the slice tokens[start:end] with the replacement_tokens list.\n\nReturn: something like {\"selected_ids\": [...], \"tokens\": [...]}. The task says \"Return selected IDs and the exact interleaved token stream.\" So we need to output the IDs in order of start? Probably the ID list as per ordering of acceptance? But the requirement: \"Order accepted calls by source start for reporting.\" So in output, selected_ids should be in order of start ascending? The description: \"Order accepted calls by source start for reporting.\" So we should sort the accepted calls by start ascending. Then apply replacements from right to left (so the order of application is reverse of start). The final token stream then matches.\n\nImplementation steps:\n- Parse case: tokens list, calls list.\n- For each call, ensure start < end.\n- Sort calls by (-score, start, call_id) as key. (call_id is string, but for tie-breaking, compare lexicographically).\n- Initialize selected = [] (list of call dicts).\n- For each call in sorted order:\n    - Check if any selected call overlaps with this call's span. Overlap if intervals intersect: not (call.end <= sel.start or sel.end <= call.start). Using exclusive end.\n    - If no overlap, add to selected.\n- Sort selected by start ascending.\n- Create result_tokens = list(tokens).\n- For call in sorted(selected, key=lambda c: c.start, reverse=True): (descending start)\n    - Replace slice: result_tokens[call.start:call.end] = call.replacement_tokens\n- Create list of selected_ids in order of start ascending (from the sorted selected).\n- Return {\"selected_ids\": selected_ids, \"tokens\": result_tokens}.\n\nEdge cases: If replacement_tokens is empty? Possibly, but we handle by replacing with empty list, which effectively removes tokens. If start==end? No span, skip? Actually calls with zero-length span would be pointless. But we can handle: if start == end, no replacement, but it would not overlap with anything? But likely not present.\n\nAlso, the fixture shows tokens are like \"t0\", \"t1\", etc., and replacement_tokens like [\"<a>\"]. So output tokens will be list of strings.\n\nWe must output a JSON object with \"implementation\" as a string containing Python source code for solve function.\n\nThe function must not mutate case, and use only standard library.\n\nLet's write the code. Ensure it's properly indented, no extra imports beyond needed. We'll import json for serialization? The function returns a JSON-serializable object (dict). No need to import json.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) as defined.\n\nAlso note: The fixture has _case_nonce, but ignore.\n\nNow produce the JSON string with implementation key.\n\nMake sure to escape backslashes, newlines, quotes in JSON string.\n\nWrite code as a single string.\n\nLet's construct:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    tokens = case[\"tokens\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    # sort by descending score, then start, then call_id\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c[\"score\"], c[\"start\"], c[\"call_id\"]))\n    selected = []\n    for call in sorted_calls:\n        start = call[\"start\"]\n        end = call[\"end\"]\n        # check overlap with any selected\n        overlap = False\n        for s in selected:\n            if not (end <= s[\"start\"] or s[\"end\"] <= start):\n                overlap = True\n                break\n        if not overlap:\n            selected.append(call)\n    # sort selected by start\n    selected_sorted = sorted(selected, key=lambda c: c[\"start\"])\n    selected_ids = [c[\"call_id\"] for c in selected_sorted]\n    # apply replacements right to left\n    result = list(tokens)\n    for call in reversed(selected_sorted):\n        start = call[\"start\"]\n        end = call[\"end\"]\n        result[start:end] = call[\"replacement_tokens\"]\n    return {\"selected_ids\": selected_ids, \"tokens\": result}\n\nBut careful: The sorted_calls uses -c[\"score\"] for descending score. That's fine.\n\nPotential issue: If multiple calls have same score and start, tie-break by call_id lexicographically. That's what Python default string comparison does.\n\nNow produce JSON string. Need to escape backslashes, double quotes, newlines.\n\nWe'll write the code as a multi-line string, then escape for JSON.\n\nLet's produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1224,"completion_tokens":1608,"total_tokens":2832,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1152},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1326},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1152,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":72},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}